WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.869
ALBERT LIN (VOICEOVER):
Maritime archaeologist

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:06.205
Garry Momber has been exploring
these waters for 20 years.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.211
Thank you.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.185
ALBERT LIN (VOICEOVER):
The English Channel

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:22.889
is a notoriously
difficult place to dive.

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:24.624
Meticulous
preparations are vital.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:32.766
Visibility isn't great.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:33.833
So be careful.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.335
We'll buddy up together.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:37.437
And I'll take you to the
bottom of the shot line,

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.573
see the seabed, and then
I'll take you to the site.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:45.178
[whistle blows]

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:47.180
Let's go!

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:48.148
Let's go!

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.987
Go!

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:54.954
They're away.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:01.260
ALBERT LIN (VOICEOVER): With
powerful tides and cold water,

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:04.730
conditions are treacherous.

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:08.234
But at the bottom of this
line, lies a treasure trove

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:09.235
from another age.

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:47.240
ALBERT LIN (VOICEOVER):
8,000 years ago,

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:50.643
sea levels were lower, and
this seabed was dry land.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.850
The wood has survived
underwater for millennia.

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:02.455
ALBERT LIN
(VOICEOVER): The trees

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:05.258
are all part of a preserved
hunter-gatherer landscape.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.198
It's a struggle
against the current

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:14.334
to get to the main site.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.803
ALBERT LIN (VOICEOVER):
We take some samples

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.873
to get a better idea of what
this is back on the boat.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.553
ALBERT LIN: That was incredible.

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:09.689
Looks like there's a
whole platform down there.

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:13.760
It's layered, one piece on top
of the other, almost like--

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:14.561
almost like a dock.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.065
Unbelievable.

00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:20.099
Ha, ha.

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:27.040
Oh, it's cold down
there and murky.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.708
[inaudible] yeah?

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.944
Ah, but it's incredible.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.513
You know, you descend
down this line.

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:38.384
And out of the darkness
comes the ancient past.

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:39.986
GARRY MOMBER: Look at that.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:43.122
Now, you can see here.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:46.858
How this piece of wood could
be locked in time in the ocean,

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.095
how does that happen,
where it doesn't decay?

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:50.363
Well, what's
happened with this

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.198
is that as the sea level
has risen above it.

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:54.534
It's covered it with this
silt, and it's taken away

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.569
all the oxygen. And
it just preserved it

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.872
in a sort of anaerobic,
oxygen-free environment.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.775
And it would have stayed there
for many more thousand years.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:05.912
But recently, the old
landscape's eroding away,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.947
which is how we found this.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:09.048
And what is that-- that--

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.116
GARRY MOMBER: This little piece?

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:11.451
Yeah, what is that?

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:12.484
Well, that little piece--
that stretched back.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:13.920
It was a little bit broken then.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:16.255
There's a peck that comes
back over the next piece.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.524
It looks like it's just a
little fix the thing in place.

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:20.727
It's composite structures
like this that is

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:21.961
of international significance.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.429
It's 8,000 years old.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:25.231
You don't just get
these every day.

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:27.100
ALBERT LIN: Can you describe
to me the scene that this

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.835
would have existed within?

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.403
This was next to a stream.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.305
And we know it was
a bit of a wetland.

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:33.806
So it could have
been a nice support.

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.074
It could have been a platform.

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.976
It could have been a pontoon.

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:38.144
We don't know yet.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.379
We don't know because
there's nothing

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.447
else like it in the country.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:42.548
Wow.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.651
And somewhere where
we found what we believe

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.652
to be the oldest
boat building site

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:47.954
in the world, where
they would have

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:49.522
built a canoe, a long boat.

00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:50.923
So that's what they
would have sailed.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:51.991
[music playing]

